# Server

`Server` matches typed routes, runs application-owned request modules and
middleware, loads route data, and returns an adapter-neutral `ServerResponse`.

```dart
import 'package:odroe/server.dart';

import 'routes.server.dart' as generated;

Server createServer() => generated.createServer();
```

## HTTP route

```dart
final route = definition.route.server(
  handlers: <HttpMethod, ServerRouteHandler<Params, NoSearch>>{
    HttpMethod.get: (context) => ServerResponse.json(
      <String, Object?>{'id': context.params.id},
    ),
  },
);
```

## Typed server function

```dart
final updatePost = ServerFunction<UpdatePost, Post>(
  id: 'posts.update',
  handler: (context) => repository.update(context.data),
);
```

An explicit `id` is the stable wire contract shared by already shipped apps and
new server deployments. Keep it unchanged after release. The compiler accepts
only a non-empty string literal and rejects duplicates across the complete route
tree. Omitting it preserves the path-and-variable fallback for gradual
migration, but renaming either source location changes that fallback.

## Authenticated calls

Authentication belongs to the application. A server-function middleware can
verify a bearer token, attach the authenticated user to the request, and return
a protocol-shaped `401` by throwing `HttpError`:

```dart
final userKey = RequestKey<User>('authenticated-user');

Future<ServerResponse> requireBearer(
  RequestContext context,
  Next next,
) async {
  final authorization = context.request.headers.value('authorization');
  final token = authorization?.startsWith('Bearer ') == true
      ? authorization!.substring('Bearer '.length)
      : null;
  final user = token == null ? null : await sessions.verify(token);
  if (user == null) throw const HttpError(401, 'Sign in required.');

  userKey.set(context, user);
  return next();
}

final updatePost = ServerFunction<UpdatePost, Post>(
  id: 'posts.update',
  middleware: <Middleware>[requireBearer],
  handler: (context) => repository.update(
    context.request.require(userKey),
    context.data,
  ),
);
```

`RequestKey` uses instance identity. Define one top-level `final` key and reuse
that exact instance when middleware stores a value and a handler reads it. Its
name is only diagnostic, so unrelated middleware may choose the same natural
name without overwriting one another. Replace old `const RequestKey(...)`
declarations with `final`, and replace `context.set(key, value)` with
`key.set(context, value)`. The key-first instance method preserves the key's
runtime value type even through a widened generic view.

`User`, `sessions`, and the token lifecycle above are application code, not
global Odroe state. On the client, `headersProvider` reads that state immediately
before every value or stream request:

```dart
final rpcModule = RpcModule.http(
  // Native apps use an explicit API origin. Omit baseUri on Web.
  baseUri: Uri.parse('https://api.example.com'),
  headersProvider: () async {
    final token = await session.validAccessToken();
    return token == null
        ? Headers()
        : Headers.single(<String, String>{
            'authorization': 'Bearer $token',
          });
  },
);
```

The provider runs exactly once for each RPC request, so a refreshed token is
used by the next call. Odroe does not automatically retry when the provider
fails or the server returns `401`; the application owns refresh, sign-in, and
any retry or idempotency policy.

Call the generated reference with the `RpcClient` installed by the module. The
`HttpError` above arrives as `RemoteServerException` with its HTTP status:

```dart
Future<Post> savePost(AppContext app, UpdatePost input) async {
  final client = app.read(rpcClientKey);
  try {
    return await routes.posts.postId.updatePost(client, input);
  } on RemoteServerException catch (error) {
    if (error.status == 401) await session.signOut();
    rethrow;
  }
}
```

## Payload budgets

RPC has four independent byte boundaries: server request input, server typed
output, client HTTP request bodies, and client typed responses. The generated
server entrypoint and default HTTP module expose each one directly:

```dart
final rpcModule = RpcModule.http(
  maxRequestBodyBytes: 2 * 1024 * 1024,
  maxResponseFrameBytes: 2 * 1024 * 1024,
);

final server = generated.createServer(
  maxFunctionPayload: 2 * 1024 * 1024,
  maxFunctionResponseFrameBytes: 2 * 1024 * 1024,
);
```

`Server.maxFunctionPayload` limits one buffered server-function request and
defaults to 1 MiB. GET payloads and request bodies use the same byte budget.
Overflow returns HTTP 413; a typed client receives a `RemoteServerException`
that preserves that status.

`Server.maxFunctionResponseFrameBytes` also defaults to 1 MiB. It counts the
UTF-8 bytes in the complete typed JSON envelope. Each stream frame receives a
fresh budget and its NDJSON line feed is excluded. Odroe encodes directly to a
bounded UTF-8 sink: an oversized value becomes a bounded `500` error frame; an
oversized stream item cancels the upstream stream after one bounded terminal
error frame. The minimum is 16 bytes so a valid `{"type":"error"}` fallback
always fits.

Malformed JSON, unknown serializer tags, and values that do not match the
generated input type return a controlled HTTP 400 before the handler starts.
They do not trigger `Server.onError`; the same exception thrown by a handler is
still an unexpected 500 and is reported.

This server limit bounds JSON bytes retained and sent by the framework, but
`Serializer.encode` still materializes one JSON-compatible object graph first.
It cannot bound objects already allocated by a handler or an unbounded
`Iterable`. Prefer pagination, multiple bounded stream items, or object storage
for large results.

`RpcClient.maxResponseFrameBytes` defaults to 1 MiB. It independently defends
clients using an older or untrusted server. A value response is one complete
frame; a stream resets the budget after each non-empty frame, so cumulative
stream size is not capped. Odroe checks bytes before UTF-8 decoding and cancels
the response body on overflow. A successful oversized response throws
`RpcProtocolException`; a non-2xx response preserves its status through
`RemoteServerException`.

The default `HttpTransport.maxRequestBodyBytes` is 10 MiB. The transport fully
buffers a body before `http.Client.send`; an exact-limit body is accepted, while
overflow cancels the source and throws the public `PayloadTooLargeException`.
This limit does not cover GET query parameters. Typed JSON has also already been
serialized before it reaches the transport, so raising the limit does not make
typed calls or uploads streaming. This exception is local and occurs before a
network request starts; GET payloads only cross the server input budget.

A function explicitly typed to return `ServerResponse` bypasses the typed frame
budget. The caller then owns streaming, limits, and body release.

## Error reporting

Unexpected module setup and rollback cleanup, middleware, handler, loader,
renderer, RPC encoding, and response-stream failures are reported through
`Server.onError`. A module setup failure is reported and rethrown with its
original stack before a response exists. Request execution failures still
return a safe `500`, typed streams still end with their bounded error frame,
and raw stream consumers still receive the original error. The default
reporter writes the error and stack trace to the current Dart `Zone`.

The generated entrypoint accepts an application reporter directly. Configure
it from the `lib/server.dart` entrypoint used by the CLI:

```dart
import 'package:odroe/server.dart';

import 'routes.server.dart' as generated;

Server createServer() => generated.createServer(
  onError: (request, error, stackTrace) => telemetry.capture(
    method: request.method.wire,
    path: request.uri.path,
    error: error,
    stackTrace: stackTrace,
  ),
);
```

The callback may finish synchronously or return a bounded `Future`. The
`Server` runtime invokes it inline, so keep work before its first asynchronous
boundary small. The returned future joins the current invocation lifetime
without holding the response. For an IO-adapter-owned failure, callback
invocation starts only after the server-side response close attempt settles;
its future is then observed by a detached request task. `HttpServer.close`
does not await that task, so telemetry that must survive process exit belongs
in an application-owned durable queue. Payload and typed-frame budget overflow, plus
normal cancellation, are always controlled and are not reported. During
request dispatch, before a response starts, `Redirect`, `NotFound`, and
`HttpError` are controlled too. Those and other errors thrown by a response
stream are reported, except typed-frame overflow. `exposeErrors` only controls
client disclosure; it does not change the original error and stack trace
delivered to `onError`. A synchronously or asynchronously failing reporter
cannot replace the original outcome and falls back to the default Zone log.

The generated native bootstrap creates one `Server` and gives both its
`handler` and `onError` to `IoServer.bind`. The same callback therefore covers
adapter-owned static files, development proxy, response metadata, HTTP
framing, and omitted-body cleanup for a raw handler. Handler and response
source-stream failures stay `Server`-owned and are not reported twice.

The generated Fetch bootstrap also creates one `Server` and gives its
`invocationHandler` and `onError` to the adapter. The adapter reports its own
status, header, `Response` construction, and response-byte conversion failures
once. Handler and source-stream failures remain `Server`-owned. A returned
reporter `Future` is registered with the host `waitUntil` without delaying the
response.

For `Server` failures, the callback receives the same `ServerRequest` for
method, path, and application trace-header correlation. A static or proxy
failure before dispatch gets a metadata-only diagnostic `ServerRequest`
without the original body. A body may otherwise already be consumed and must
not be read again. For a supported method, malformed forwarded authority is
rejected with a controlled `400` before static or proxy dispatch and does not
call the application reporter. Before response commit, invalid
metadata is discarded with the old status, reason, headers, cookies, and
framing before a fixed `500` is written. After commit, a stream failure can
only close the connection. The framework itself adds only method and path to
the default log, not query, headers, or body. The error and stack trace are
written verbatim, so applications must not put sensitive data in exceptions.

`ServerInvocation.onError` is the separate adapter-lifetime fallback for
background and cleanup failures that no host owns, plus synchronous host
`waitUntil` registration failures. Once a host accepts a task, the host owns
any later rejection.

## Cancellation

Value and stream references accept one application-owned cancellation signal.
Completing it stops the call with `RpcCancelledException`:

```dart
final title = await routes.posts.postId.readTitle(
  app.read(rpcClientKey),
  postId,
  cancelled: cancelButton.future,
);
```

A pre-cancelled call does not start its `headersProvider` or transport. If an
asynchronous provider is still running, the RPC stops waiting and no request is
sent. `RpcClient` stops typed value and stream consumption and cancels the
upstream subscription. The default `HttpTransport` additionally aborts request
bodies, response setup, and response bodies. A custom `RpcTransport` must
observe `ServerRequest.cancelled` to release the I/O it owns. A caller-supplied
`http.Client` may not support the physical abort trigger, but a typed call still
ends promptly.

The same signal can express a total timeout without a second API:

```dart
final title = await routes.posts.postId.readTitle(
  app.read(rpcClientKey),
  postId,
  cancelled: Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 10)),
);
```

Odroe does not choose total, setup, or stream-idle timeout semantics and does
not retry a cancelled call. Aborting a POST does not roll back work that the
server already started. A handler that can stop cooperatively should observe
its underlying `ServerRequest.cancelled`; an adapter can only report a remote
disconnect when its runtime exposes one.

Browser RPC is same-origin only. Omit `baseUri` on Web so
`RpcModule.http()` uses `Uri.base`; a separate Web application and API origin
requires an application-owned same-origin proxy. Android, iOS, and desktop apps
may pass an explicit `baseUri` because browser CORS and credential rules do not
apply to their native HTTP transport. The official app example reads
`ODROE_API_ORIGIN` from `--dart-define` for native targets and rejects a missing
or non-HTTP(S) origin instead of silently using a non-network `Uri.base`; add
the desired Flutter runner to that application before selecting a native
device.

The generated client and server manifest agree on the identifier and wire
types. Request and typed response budgets are independent and explicit at
their owning server or client boundary.
Server, route, middleware, and server-function configuration is copied into
immutable snapshots, so runtime matching cannot drift after construction.
Unsupported transport methods return `501`; matched routes return `405` with
their supported methods in `Allow`.

## Runtime boundary

`server_io.dart` adapts the contract to `dart:io`. Fetch-style runtimes use a
separate adapter so shared server code does not import JavaScript interop.
Both adapters release response bodies that HTTP semantics omit. The Fetch
adapter also cancels unread request streams when a handler throws or the
response ends, is cancelled, or is omitted.
Cloudflare Workers that run this Fetch adapter must enable the
`enable_request_signal` compatibility flag for a real incoming client
disconnect to abort `Request.signal` and complete `ServerRequest.cancelled`. A
synthetic Node `AbortController` test does not enable that runtime behavior.
